For airplanes with four or more engines, the steady rate of climb at any expected operating altitude, and at any weight within the range of weights specified in the Airplane Flight Manual, must be determined with the--
(a) Landing gear retracted;
(b) Wing flaps in the most favorable position;
(c) Cowl flaps (or other means of controlling the engine cooling air supply) in the position that provides adequate cooling in the hot-day condition;
(d) The most unfavorable center of gravity;
(e) Two critical engines on one side of the airplane inoperative and their propellers stopped; and
(f) Remaining engines at the maximum continuous power available at each altitude.
